{
  "image_name": "eab6957ca75a3d69942b280f18b1c0d9.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes an electrochemical system that couples hydrogen peroxide generation with biomass or plastic-derived oxidation, and contrasts it with more conventional anodic reactions. Overall, it shows a membrane-separated flow cell in which oxygen is reduced at a gas-diffusion cathode through a two-electron ORR pathway to form H2O2, while the anode carries out value-added oxidation instead of oxygen evolution.\n\nOne schematic shows furfural in alkaline solution being oxidized on a Ni foam anode to 2-furoic acid, while oxygen fed to the cathode is converted to H2O2. A second panel places this concept on an energy diagram, indicating that pairing 2e− ORR with HMF oxidation to 2,5-furandicarboxylic acid requires a lower cell voltage than pairing ORR with OER, and it is also compared with FAOR and EOR.\n\nAnother panel extends the idea to PET upcycling: PET-derived species such as EG and PTA appear to undergo anodic oxidation to products including formate and PTA, while the cathode still produces H2O2, with downstream distillation and KDF separation indicated.\n\nThe final schematic presents a practical paired-electrolyzer layout with separate cathode and anode circulation loops, an anion-exchange membrane, and Ov-Bi2O3-based electrodes, emphasizing simultaneous H2O2 production and selective anodic upgrading.",
